<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05608v1/gpt-eval.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:228/113;" width="228" height="113"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plan Quality Analysis</span>.
The comparison of GPT-4.1-generated and our plans on ALFWorld.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Impact of Plan Insertion Position.</span>
We further investigate the impact of different insertion positions on performance: in the task instruction (used in our main experiments), in the executor’s thought process, and in the environment observation.
Shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we find that although different insertion positions can all lead to performance improvements, insertion into the task instruction consistently yields the best performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization Across Executor Agents.</span>
We explore the impact of different executor backbones and prompting strategies shown in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3 Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
We can observe that our method is effective not only across different executor backbones (Llama-3.1 vs. Qwen-2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), but also brings performance improvements to models with different parameter sizes (Llama-3.1-8B vs. Llama-3.1-70B).
Similarly, our method is compatible with different prompting strategies.
We can find that when the executor model uses different prompting strategies like Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, our method can also lead to significant performance improvements.</p>
</div>
