<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the recent progress in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, researchers attempt to use prompting techniques to build agents that can handle real-world tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, e.g., writing code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and web navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chae et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, these agents with static workflow struggle with planning hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, leading to brainless trial-and-error in the environment.
Thus, some recent works attempt to improve the planning capabilities of LLM-based agents by applying the SFT stage on collected expert trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, while others enable agents to interact with the environment and leverage reinforcement learning to learn from failed explorations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fei et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, these <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implicit planning methods</span> require agents to learn from collected data or agent-environment interactions implicitly, and focus solely on local planning, i.e, the planning that occurs through interleaved reasoning and action generation, limiting the global planning ability for long-horizon tasks.
Also, implicit planning methods are generally inefficient: SFT-based methods are data-inefficient, requiring substantial amounts of expert-annotated data to generalize well due to limited exploration of environments; RL-based methods are training-inefficient, requiring extensive training time and iterations to converge, as agent-environment interactions involve many decision steps, sparse rewards, and long-term credit assignment.
Such implicit methods often require retraining each time a new agent is deployed, and need a large amount of expert-annotated trajectories or more training iterations to converge, making them inefficient.
To address the challenges of myopic reasoning and planning hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, some works use language models to synthesize task-related knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, but the generated knowledge is static and cannot be further optimized through agent-environment feedback, leading to suboptimal performance.
Recently, some approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have explored using explicit knowledge to guide task execution.
However, these <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explicit planning methods</span> often require manually designed training data or manually constructed knowledge bases, making them difficult to transfer across different environments.
In contrast, our proposed <span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Eaglet</span> presents a novel framework that is both efficient and effective by training a global planner through a fully automated pipeline, thus removing the dependency on manual efforts.
This results in a plug-and-play planner that provides global foresight to mitigate planning hallucinations for any new executor agent, therefore achieving state-of-the-art performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Dataset Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScienceWorld.</span>
ScienceWorld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is a text-based virtual environment designed as a rigorous evaluation platform, with a specific focus on assessing and advancing scientific reasoning capabilities.
This environment enables researchers to systematically measure the performance of agents within open-ended, complex scenarios.
ScienceWorld includes tasks derived from standard elementary science curricula, spanning topics such as phase transitions, measurement, electricity, life sciences, plant development, chemical reactions, and genetics. Agents are placed in an embodied, interactive setting, requiring them to comprehend and operationalize sophisticated scientific concepts.
Each task in ScienceWorld is composed of multiple subgoals, and final rewards are determined based on the achievement of these subgoals.
ScienceWorld test set features previously unseen task variants—for instance, while training tasks might require boiling water, test tasks may involve boiling lead.
In line with prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we utilize the original test set with 241 samples to assess the generalization capacity of our planner in unseen scenarios, and employ the original validation set with 194 samples to evaluate performance in seen settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALFWorld.</span>
ALFWorld encompasses six categories of planning tasks set primarily in home environments, covering not only basic object manipulation (such as pick and place) but also tasks requiring complex interaction sequences.
For example, the heating task requires models to first identify target objects, move them to heating devices (like a microwave), execute the heating operation, and finally place them in designated locations to complete the task.
Following prior research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we evaluate model performance under two conditions: seen and unseen scenarios.
Seen scenarios consist of 140 task instances from rooms encountered during training; unseen scenarios comprise 134 task instances from entirely new rooms with container arrangements and scene organizations distinctly different from training tasks, designed to evaluate the model’s zero-shot generalization capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WebShop.</span>
WebShop is a complex, web-based interactive environment designed to test the LLM agents in realistic online shopping scenarios.
To complete the task, the agent must interact with a simulated HTML-based shopping website to search for, navigate to, and ultimately purchase a suitable item.
Once the agent clicks the "buy" option, the environment provides a final reward, which is calculated based on the matching heuristics of the product’s attributes and price.
It contains over 1.1 million products, providing a rich and diverse action space.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we use the same 200 samples to conduct the experiments to make a fair comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
