<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>
<span id="A3.T8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Success Rates.</span>
We report the success rates on ScienceWorld and WebShop benchmarks.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters and Devices.</span>
For SFT training, we use the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba, 2017</a>)</cite> to train our model, with a <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> learning rate with a weight decay of 0.1, and a batch size of 16, steering the training across 3 epochs.
We conduct our SFT stage with DeepSpeed+ZeRO3 and BF16.
For RL training, each episode allows up to 30 environment steps for ALFWorld and WebShop.
Each episode allows up to 50 environment steps for ScienceWorld during the RL training.
The learning rate is set to <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> for the actor.
For EAGLET, we use a group size <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of 8, and the rollout temperature is set to 1.0, while the validation temperature is set to 0.4.
The mini-batch size is 128, the KL-divergence loss coefficient <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.01, and <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.2.
The gamma-decay factor <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.2.
For each task, we use the checkpoint from step 50 for testing.
For baseline GiGPO, we retrain it based on the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct backbone as GiGPO-Llama-3.1-8B (i.e., GiGPO in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Global Planning Policy Optimization ‣ 3.2 Global Planner RL Training ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which is different from the original paper.
GiGPO-Llama-3.1-8B is more difficult to converge compared to our method EAGLET, we use the checkpoint from step 400 for testing.
During the homologous consensus filtering and computing executor capability gain reward, we select Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and GiGPO-Llama-3.1-8B as homologous executors and apply the same inference prompts as the evaluation phase.
Executor agents w/ training baselines in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Global Planning Policy Optimization ‣ 3.2 Global Planner RL Training ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are implemented on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Experiments are conducted on NVIDIA A100 80G GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span>
During the inference, each episode is limited to 30 environment steps for ALFWorld and WebShop.
In ScienceWorld, the number of steps ranges from 10 to 120 depending on the task type, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, with an average of around 40.
We also report the success rate as shown in Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix C Implementation and Evaluation Details ‣ A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
We can find that our method not only achieves state-of-the-art average reward results, but also achieves better success rates.
We infer our planner twice and infer the executor agent based on the generated plan twice, for a total of four times, to obtain stable results.
For baselines, we infer them twice to report the final results, e.g., MPO+GPT-5, or directly use the results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
For GPT-5, we utilize <span id="A3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-5-2025-08-07</span> to get the results.
For GPT-4.1, we utilize <span id="A3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-4.1-2025-04-14</span> to get the results.
For DeepSeek-V3.1-Think and DeepSeek-V3.1-Non-Think, we separately use <span id="A3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepseek-reasoner</span> and <span id="A3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deepseek-chat</span> to obtain the results.</p>
</div>
